  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-07-south-bronx/">← Back to District 7 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 7 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 7. Navigating the special education system can be daunting. This directory provides essential contact information, but also crucial advice on communication protocols and escalation strategies to ensure your child receives the appropriate support and services.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 1 (Serving District 7)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>1 Fordham Plaza, Bronx, NY 10458</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-329-8001">718-329-8001</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Remember to keep thorough records of all communication attempts, including dates, times, names of individuals contacted, and summaries of conversations. This documentation will be crucial if you need to file a formal complaint.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is crucial for triggering the district's obligations under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. This creates a written record of your conversation and requests.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em>. A clear subject line helps ensure your email is properly routed and addressed promptly.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. This log should include the date, time, method of communication (phone, email, in-person), the name of the person you communicated with, and a brief summary of the conversation.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request and the reason for contacting them.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). CC'ing the principal demonstrates the importance of the issue and can expedite a response. Reiterate your initial request and mention that you are seeking assistance in ensuring your child receives appropriate special education services.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. If you still haven't received a response, escalate to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. In your email, explain the situation, the previous attempts to contact the CSE, and the lack of response. Clearly state the specific action you are requesting.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied. According to IDEA and NYS Part 200 Regulations, parents have the right to file a formal state complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) if they believe the school district has violated their child's rights under IDEA. This complaint must be filed within one year of the alleged violation (8 NYCRR §200.5(l)).</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 7
    </h2>

    <div class="advocacy-tip-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="advocacy-tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Rights</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. Understanding your rights is the first step to effective advocacy.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Review the "Parent's Guide to Special Education in New York State."</li>
                <li>Understand the IEP process and your right to participate meaningfully. (8 NYCRR §200.4)</li>
                <li>Know your right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) if you disagree with the school's evaluation. (8 NYCRR §200.5(g))</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain a comprehensive file of all communication, evaluations, IEPs, and other relevant documents. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child or file a complaint.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Create a digital and/or physical file system.</li>
                <li>Keep copies of all emails, letters, and reports.</li>
                <li>Record dates and details of all meetings and phone calls.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Prepare for IEP Meetings</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                IEP meetings are crucial opportunities to advocate for your child's needs. Prepare thoroughly to ensure your voice is heard.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Review your child's IEP and identify areas for improvement.</li>
                <li>Prepare a list of questions and concerns.</li>
                <li>Bring data or observations to support your requests.</li>
                <li>Consider bringing a support person (e.g., advocate, therapist) to the meeting.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Build Relationships</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Building positive relationships with school staff can facilitate collaboration and improve outcomes for your child.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Communicate regularly with your child's teachers and therapists.</li>
                <li>Attend school events and parent-teacher conferences.</li>
                <li>Be respectful and professional in your interactions.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="common-pitfalls" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District 7 Pitfalls (and How to Avoid Them)
    </h2>

    <div class="pitfall-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Delayed Evaluations</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Districts sometimes delay initial evaluations or re-evaluations, violating the 60-day timeline mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii).
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Submit your request for evaluation in writing (certified mail is recommended). Track the 60-day timeline carefully. If the deadline is approaching and the evaluation has not been completed, send a follow-up letter demanding compliance. If necessary, file a state complaint with NYSED.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Inadequate IEP Goals</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                IEP goals that are not specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) can hinder your child's progress.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Advocate for SMART goals at IEP meetings. Provide specific examples of areas where your child needs support and propose measurable goals that align with their needs. If you disagree with the proposed goals, document your concerns in the IEP and request a meeting to revise them. You can also request mediation.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Denial of Related Services</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The district may deny related services (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling) that your child needs to benefit from their education.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Obtain documentation from qualified professionals (e.g., doctors, therapists) supporting the need for the related services. Present this documentation at the IEP meeting and advocate strongly for their inclusion in the IEP. If the services are denied, request prior written notice explaining the reasons for the denial, and consider requesting an impartial hearing.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Lack of Parent Involvement</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
               The district may not adequately involve parents in the IEP process, violating IDEA's emphasis on parent participation.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Actively participate in all IEP meetings and communication with the school. Document all attempts to communicate with the school and any instances where your input was not valued. If you feel your voice is not being heard, consider bringing an advocate or attorney to IEP meetings.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What is the difference between a CSE and a CPSE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) handles special education services for students aged 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) handles services for children aged 3-5. Because this page focuses on District 7, and District 7 schools generally serve children 5 and older, the focus is on the CSE.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What if I disagree with the school's evaluation of my child?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">You have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)). You must request the IEE in writing and explain why you disagree with the school's evaluation. The school district must either pay for the IEE or initiate an impartial hearing to demonstrate that its evaluation was appropriate.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What is an IEP and what should it include?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">An Individualized Education Program (IEP) is a written document that outlines the special education and related services a child with a disability needs to receive a free and appropriate public education (FAPE). The IEP must include (8 NYCRR §200.4(d)(2)):</p>
        <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>Present levels of performance</li>
            <li>Measurable annual goals</li>
            <li>Special education and related services</li>
            <li>Program modifications and accommodations</li>
            <li>Placement recommendations</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What is Prior Written Notice (PWN) and when should I receive it?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Prior Written Notice (PWN) is a written explanation that the school district must provide to parents whenever it proposes to take (or refuses to take) an action regarding the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of a child with a disability (8 NYCRR §200.5(a)). You should receive PWN whenever the school district makes a decision about your child's special education services.</p>
    </div>

</section>

    </div>
</main>
